    "what_is_it": {
      "type": "pp-image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_1": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Here's What's Actually Happening Inside Your Eyes",
            "heading_size": "h0",
            "heading_alignment": "left",
            "heading_mobilealignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#14312d"
          }
        },
        "text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>During perimenopause, your estrogen and androgen levels (hormones like DHEA) don't just dip once — they swing, unevenly, sometimes for years. Those hormones aren't just involved in hot flashes and mood. They also help keep your meibomian glands (which make the oily layer of your tears) and your lacrimal glands (which make the watery layer) working the way they're supposed to.</p><p>When that hormonal support falls away, your tear film gets thinner and evaporates faster than it should. The result is what's called evaporative dry eye — eyes that feel gritty, burning, or exhausted, even though they can look completely normal in the mirror, or even watery. That gap, between how your eyes look and how they actually feel, is part of why it's so often dismissed — even by the people closest to you.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_alignment": "left",
            "text_mobilealignment": "left",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["heading_1", "text_1"],
      "settings": {
        "image": "",
        "desktop_image_width": "medium",
