    "emotional_impact": {
      "type": "pp-image-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_1": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "It's Not Just Your Eyes — It's Your Whole Day",
            "heading_size": "h0",
            "heading_alignment": "left",
            "heading_mobilealignment": "center",
            "heading_color": "#14312d"
          }
        },
        "text_1": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>It's blinking hard through a work call so no one notices your eyes stinging. It's putting a book down because the words have gone blurry, not because the story lost you. It's driving at night and quietly bracing for oncoming headlights. It's someone saying \"have you tried just resting your eyes?\" when you've already tried that, four times today.</p><p>Dry eye doesn't stay contained to your eyes. It touches your focus, your mood, your sleep — even how confident you feel walking into a room with eyes that look red or glassy no matter how much rest you got. And because so much of it is invisible from the outside, it can feel like you're carrying it alone.</p><p>You're not, and you don't have to just wait it out. Perimenopausal dry eye is well understood medically, and there's a lot you can genuinely do to support your comfort day to day.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_alignment": "left",
            "text_mobilealignment": "left",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["heading_1", "text_1"],
      "settings": {
        "image": "",
        "desktop_image_width": "medium",
        "image_border_radius": 25,
        "layout": "text_first",
        "mobile_layout": "image_first",
        "gradient": "",
