    "faq": {
      "type": "pp-faq",
      "blocks": {
        "question_1": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Is perimenopausal dry eye permanent?",
            "heading_size": "h2",
            "heading_color": "#14312d",
            "text": "<p>For many women, symptoms ease once hormone levels stabilize after menopause — but for others, dry eye becomes an ongoing part of eye care. Either way, the goal is comfort and support, not just waiting it out.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        },
        "question_2": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Can supplements and self-care actually help?",
            "heading_size": "h2",
            "heading_color": "#14312d",
            "text": "<p>Omega-3 fatty acids are one of the most studied nutritional supports for tear film quality, and simple habits like cooling compresses and blinking breaks can meaningfully ease day-to-day discomfort. They work best alongside — not instead of — guidance from an eye care professional.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        },
        "question_3": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "How long before I notice a difference?",
            "heading_size": "h2",
            "heading_color": "#14312d",
            "text": "<p>Comfort routines are cumulative. Many people notice small improvements within a few weeks of consistent use, though everyone's eyes — and hormones — respond differently.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        },
        "question_4": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Should I still see a doctor?",
            "heading_size": "h2",
            "heading_color": "#14312d",
            "text": "<p>Yes. If your symptoms are severe, sudden, or affecting your vision, please see an optometrist or ophthalmologist. Dewy is here to support day-to-day comfort, not to diagnose or treat any medical condition.</p>",
            "text_style": "subtitle",
            "text_color": "#3f5854"
          }
        }
      },
